# MV(1)

## NAME

mv - move or rename files and directories

## SYNOPSIS

| **mv** file1 file2

| **mv** dir1 dir2

| **mv** file ... directory

## DESCRIPTION

*Mv* moves (changes the name of) *file1* to *file2*.

If *file2* already exists, it is removed before *file1* is moved. If *file2* has a mode which
forbids writing, *mv* prints the mode (see chmod(2)) and reads the standard input to obtain a
line; if the line begins with **y,** the move takes place; if not, *mv* exits. The question is
asked only when the standard input is a terminal.

**Note:** the second SYNOPSIS line is an addition. The v7 page showed two forms and the program
has always had three - its own usage message says **mv f1 f2; or mv d1 d2; or mv f1 ... fn d1**
\- and the third of them, renaming a **directory**, is the whole of what makes *mv* interesting
on this machine. See below.

In the second form, one or more *files* are moved to the *directory* with their original
file-names.

*Mv* refuses to move a file onto itself.

## RENAMING A DIRECTORY

**Note:** this section is new; v7's page did not mention the case at all.

There is no *rename* system call on this system, and no *mkdir* or *rmdir* either. A directory
is renamed by hand. When the old and the new name share a parent that is a link(2) and an
unlink(2) and no more, because `..` already points where it should. When they do **not** share
a parent it takes four calls - link the new name, unlink the old, unlink the directory's own
`..`, and link the new parent in its place - and until all four have run the filesystem is
inconsistent. *Mv* ignores every signal across that sequence for exactly that reason.

**Note: /bin/mv is set-user-id root, and has to be.** Both link(2) and unlink(2) refuse a
directory to anyone but the super-user (**if ((ip->i_mode & IFMT) == IFDIR && !suser())**,
kernel/sys2.c and kernel/sys4.c), so none of the calls above can be made by an ordinary user.
The bit is `mode 04755` in root.manifest, and mkdir(1) and rmdir(1) carry it for the same kind
of reason.

**Root is borrowed and not granted.** *Mv* calls **setuid(getuid())** as soon as it knows the
argument is not a directory, so every other form of *mv* runs with the invoking user's own
identity and no privilege whatever. For the directory form the privilege is kept, and what
decides whether the rename is allowed is *mv*'s own access(2) checks on both parents and on the
directory itself - access(2) asking about the **real** user id. So the answer is the one the
user would have got without the setuid bit; only the mechanics need root.

Two renames are refused outright:

A directory may not be moved **into itself** or into anything below it. *Mv* walks from the
target's parent up to the root through `..` and stops if it meets the source.

A path that spells **..** in any component is refused, rather than reasoned about. (This test
is reached only when the two parents differ; a rename inside one parent takes the simpler
branch and never looks.)

## DIAGNOSTICS

**Note:** v7's page had no DIAGNOSTICS section. All of these go to the standard error and *mv*
exits 1.

**mv: cannot access** *file* - the source could not be stat'ed.

**mv: directory rename only** - a directory appeared among the arguments of a multi-file move.
Renaming a directory takes exactly two arguments.

**mv:** *file* **is a directory**

**mv:** *file* **and** *file* **are identical**

**mv: cannot unlink** *file*

**mv:** *dir*\&**/**\&*name*\&**:** *name too long* and **mv: target name too long** - a
constructed name would not fit 100 characters. v7 checked one of the three places this can
happen and did not bound the other two.

**mv:** *dir* **exists** - the target of a directory rename is in the way.

**mv: cannot rename** *name* - the last component of the source is `.`, `..` or empty.

**mv: cannot locate parent**

**mv: no write access to** *dir* - and this is the check that asks about the **real** user id;
see above.

**mv: cannot move directories across devices**

**mv: cannot move a directory into itself**

**mv: Sorry, path names including .. aren't allowed**

**mv: cannot link** *a* **to** *b* and **mv:** *file*\&**: cannot unlink** - one of the four
calls of a re-parent failed. *Mv* undoes what it can and says which call it was.

**mv: try again** - the fork(2) for a cross-device copy failed.

**mv: cannot exec cp** and **mv: cannot wait for cp** - see BUGS.

## BUGS

If *file1* and *file2* lie on different file systems, *mv* must copy the file and delete the
original. In this case the owner name becomes that of the copying process and any linking
relationship with other files is lost.

**Note:** `must copy the file` is literal - *mv fork*\&s and *exec*\&s **/bin/cp**, by that
name and with no search of any kind, so the move fails outright if *cp* is not on the
filesystem. It is not as bad as the paragraph above suggests, though: the times **are** kept,
because *mv* restores them with utime(2) after the copy returns. Only the owner and the links
are lost.

**This became reachable when /usr moved onto a pack of its own.** A cross-device move is one
whose link(2) came back **EXDEV**, and *mv /usr/bin/cc /bin/cc* is now exactly that: */usr* is
a second filesystem, mounted by */etc/rc* on **/dev/md1**. The path is faithfully ported and
**/bin/cp** really is on the image, but nothing in the test suite exercises it yet.

*Mv* should take **-f** flag, like *rm,* to suppress the question if the target exists and is
not writable.

## SEE ALSO

cp(1), ln(1), rm(1), chmod(2), link(2), unlink(2), utime(2)
